input.message:
Thank you for your update (13 June, 11:27) confirming a small drip when the kitchen tap was used and that you have not touched the hatch.

Where things stand: the plumber attended on 13 June in the agreed 10:30–12:30 window and stopped before opening the ceiling because an unidentified pale lining is visible behind the ceiling board and we hold no survey confirming what it is. The pipe repair is still needed, but it cannot go ahead until that material has been identified by a specialist. We are arranging that now. I will not give you a date until an appointment is actually booked and confirmed with you.

In the meantime, please:
- do not open or touch the ceiling hatch, and do not drill, scrape or disturb the kitchen ceiling or the stained area;
- keep the area directly under the damp patch clear, and keep children away from it;
- use the kitchen tap as little as possible; if you must use it, run it only briefly and place a bowl or container under the patch to catch drips, and dry any water on worktops or the floor;
- do not place anything electrical under or near the patch.

Please contact us straight away — and treat it as an emergency out of hours — if the ceiling starts to sag or bulge, the crack widens, pieces of ceiling come away, dripping becomes continuous, or water appears near any light fitting, socket or switch.

I will come back to you as soon as the next appointment is confirmed.
